  "question": {
    "text": "Consider a function&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;defined by&nbsp;<math><mi>f</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo></math> then"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>f</mi></math> is one-one but not onto",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>f</mi></math> is onto but not one-one",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>f</mi></math> is one-one and onto",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>f</mi></math> is neither one-one nor onto",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given,&nbsp;<math><mi>f</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math><br />Now,&nbsp;<math> <mrow> <msup> <mi>f</mi> <mo>′</mo> </msup> <mfenced> <mi>x</mi> </mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msup> <mi>x</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msup> <mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow> </math><br /><math><mo>∴</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is strictly increasing function<br /><math><mo>∴</mo></math> It is one-one<br />Clearly, <math><mi>f</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is a continuous function and also increasing on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>-</mo><mo>∞</mo></mrow></munder><mi>f</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>∞</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>∞</mo></mrow></munder><mi>f</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>∞</mi></math><br /><math><mo>∴</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> takes every value between <math><mo>-</mo><mi>∞</mi></math> and <math><mi>∞</mi><mo>.</mo></math><br />Thus, <math><mi>f</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is an onto function."
  }
}